RD.push([6,"By the time the ferry’s engines died, the island had already stopped being a place and become a rumour. Fog lay so heavily on the jetty that planks appeared one at a time under Lena’s boots and vanished behind her. Somewhere ahead a bell rang, thin and patient, like someone clearing their throat. She had come for a weekend. She had told herself so on the mainland, and again on the boat. Now, with the cold seeping through her gloves, the promise sounded like something a stranger had said.",[["The writer describes the planks appearing one at a time mainly to","show how little Lena can see around her",["suggest the jetty is falling apart","show that Lena is walking slowly out of fear","emphasise how long the jetty is"],"Purpose of a Detail"],["Calling the island a “rumour” suggests it seemed","uncertain and half imaginary",["famous for its stories","crowded with gossip","newly discovered"],"Writer's Technique"],["Which pair best describes Lena’s attitude at the start of the journey and at the end of the passage?","she reassures herself; she doubts her decision",["she is excited; she is amused by the fog","she fears missing the ferry; she is relieved to arrive","she is bored by the crossing; she is curious about the bell"],"Change in Feeling"]]],
[7,"Mr Okafor has repaired watches in the arcade for forty years, and the arcade has grown up around him like ivy around a post. Shops come and go; his stall, a cabinet barely wider than its owner, stays. He works with a jeweller’s loupe screwed into one eye, and when customers speak he lifts a single finger, as though the conversation must wait its turn behind the tick of a mechanism. Most people find this rude until they see the watch again, running, and understand that he was listening all along, to something else.",[["The comparison with ivy suggests that Mr Okafor","has become a fixed and natural part of the arcade",["is slowly harming the other shops","grows harder to deal with as he ages","depends on the other traders for support"],"Writer's Technique"],["The writer mentions the raised finger mainly to show that Mr Okafor","gives the work his full attention first",["dislikes his customers","cannot hear them properly","is about to speak"],"Purpose of a Detail"],["How does the customers’ attitude towards him change?","from irritation to understanding",["from curiosity to boredom","from admiration to doubt","from fear to affection"],"Change in Feeling"]]]);
