const PSG=[];
PSG.push({id:'lamp',t:'The Lamplighter’s Ledger',lv:4,ps:[
`When Ines inherited the ledger, she assumed it was a record of accounts: a dull inheritance for a dull winter. It was bound in cracked calfskin, and its pages, close-written in a hand so cramped it seemed to be apologising for its own existence, were crowded with columns of dates, street names and what appeared to be weather. Her grandfather had been a lamplighter long after the council had begun to replace gas with electricity, and Ines had always privately considered his devotion to the profession a form of stubbornness dressed as loyalty.`,
`It was not until the third evening that she noticed the pattern. Beside each street name, in the margin, he had recorded not the number of lamps but the number of windows that were lit as he passed. The figures rose through autumn, dwindled in the dead weeks after midwinter, and surged again in spring. Here, unmistakably, was not a ledger of labour but a census of company: he had been counting, night after night, the evidence that the town was still awake.`,
`She read on, half reluctant, half rapt. Here and there an entry broke from its terse formula. “Number 14, curtains drawn for the first time since the widow’s funeral; lit the lamp outside a little brighter.” “Boy at the corner house practising the cornet; flat, but persevering; slowed my round to listen.” The man she remembered as taciturn, even forbidding, emerged from these notations as a connoisseur of other people’s small perseverances, a witness who had never once been thanked because nobody knew he was watching.`,
`On the last page the writing grew shakier, and the final entry bore no street name at all. “Counted my own window tonight,” it said. “Lit. Is that enough?” Ines sat for a long while with the book open on her knees. Outside, the street lamps, electric and indifferent, hummed into life one by one, and she found, to her considerable surprise, that she had gone to the window to count.`],
q:[
['Inference',`In paragraph 1, Ines’s attitude towards her grandfather’s career is best characterised as`,`dismissive, since she mistook his perseverance for mere obstinacy`,[`reverential, because she admired his skill with the lamps`,`bewildered, since she could not fathom his daily routines`,`resentful, because the work had kept him from his family`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The phrase “so cramped it seemed to be apologising for its own existence” (paragraph 1) primarily suggests that the handwriting was`,`diffident and tightly compressed`,[`ostentatious and florid`,`illegible through haste`,`deliberately encrypted`]],
['Inference',`What do the marginal figures in paragraph 2 reveal about what her grandfather truly recorded?`,`the extent to which the town remained inhabited and alert after dark`,[`the efficiency of the council’s replacement lamps`,`the seasonal consumption of gas`,`the number of customers who owed him payment`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The quoted entries in paragraph 3 serve mainly to`,`reveal a perceptiveness and tenderness at odds with Ines’s memory of him`,[`demonstrate that the lamplighter neglected his official duties`,`illustrate the musical education available to the town’s children`,`prove that the ledger had been falsified`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 3, “taciturn” most nearly means`,`habitually uncommunicative`,[`perpetually argumentative`,`excessively frugal`,`visibly melancholic`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`In the final sentence, Ines’s decision to “count” most plausibly signifies that she has`,`come to share the sentiment that animated his vigils`,[`resolved to resume his profession`,`grown suspicious of her neighbours`,`accepted that electric light is superior to gas`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'reef',t:'The Slow Engineering of Reefs',lv:5,ps:[
`To the casual snorkeller, a coral reef presents itself as a garden: an exuberant, motionless profusion of colour. This impression, though pleasing, is almost wholly mistaken. A reef is not a plant community but an animal one, and its apparent stillness conceals a ceaseless commerce of chemicals, light and waste that biologists have spent the better part of a century attempting to untangle.`,
`The foundation of that commerce is a partnership. Coral polyps, soft-bodied creatures no larger than a lentil, shelter within their tissues microscopic algae that harvest sunlight. In return for lodging, the algae furnish the polyp with sugars, supplying by some estimates up to ninety per cent of its energy. The arrangement is so efficient that reefs flourish in tropical waters that are, paradoxically, almost devoid of nutrients, like a thriving metropolis sited in a desert.`,
`Yet the partnership is precarious. When the water warms by even a degree or two above the seasonal maximum, the algae begin to produce compounds that are toxic to their hosts, and the polyps expel them. Stripped of their pigmented lodgers, the corals turn a bleached, skeletal white. Bleaching is not in itself death; a reef that experiences a brief spell of heat may recover as the algae gradually return. But repeated episodes, with too little interval for recuperation, can transform a convalescence into a terminal decline.`,
`Some researchers, accordingly, have begun to investigate whether corals can be assisted towards resilience, either by selecting heat-tolerant colonies for propagation or by cultivating hardier strains of algae. Sceptics counter that such interventions are palliative rather than curative, addressing a symptom while the underlying warming proceeds unchecked. The dispute is unlikely to be resolved soon; what is beyond dispute is that the reef’s silence has been deceptive, and that its history is far more eventful than its surface suggests.`],
q:[
['Purpose of a Detail',`The central claim of paragraph 1 is that`,`a reef’s tranquil appearance misrepresents its biological complexity`,[`snorkellers seldom appreciate the colour of coral`,`reefs are more closely related to plants than to animals`,`biologists have already unravelled the chemistry of reefs`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 2, “furnish” most nearly means`,`supply`,[`embellish`,`borrow`,`demand`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The comparison of a reef to “a thriving metropolis sited in a desert” is intended to emphasise that`,`abundant life persists despite scarce nutrients in the surrounding water`,[`reefs are crowded with competing species`,`tropical water is dangerously saline`,`coral cannot survive without human settlement nearby`]],
['Literal Comprehension',`According to paragraph 3, bleaching becomes fatal chiefly when`,`heat episodes recur before the coral has had time to recover`,[`the algae multiply beyond the polyp’s capacity to host them`,`the polyps abandon the reef of their own accord`,`the water temperature falls below its seasonal minimum`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The author’s stance towards the “sceptics” in paragraph 4 is best described as`,`even-handed, presenting their objection without endorsing it`,[`dismissive of their objections as uninformed`,`wholly persuaded by their reasoning`,`hostile to the researchers’ proposals`]],
['Inference',`Which statement is most strongly supported by the final sentence?`,`Scientific study has revealed activity that a casual observer would not detect`,[`Reefs have recently become more eventful than they once were`,`The disagreement between the researchers has now been settled`,`Silence is a reliable sign of ecological health`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'archive',t:'In Defence of the Unimportant Document',lv:6,ps:[
`Archivists are fond of recounting the story of the grocery receipt: a crumpled slip, retained by an accident of thrift in the lining of a coat, that later enabled a historian to date a famous event to within a single afternoon. The tale is told with an air of mild triumph, as though to rebuke those who would discard whatever fails to announce its significance. Yet its moral is less straightforward than it appears.`,
`The difficulty is that no institution can keep everything. Every act of preservation is therefore, simultaneously, an act of selection, and selection is never neutral. The criteria by which a generation judges a document worthy of retention reflect its own preoccupations: treaties and speeches survive because statecraft was esteemed; the letters of servants and the ledgers of small shops perish because they were not. Posterity, in short, inherits not the past but the past as filtered through the prejudices of its custodians.`,
`It would be easy to conclude from this that the archive is a monument to bias and ought to be distrusted. That conclusion, however, is as facile as the credulity it opposes. A filtered record is still a record; its gaps can themselves be read, much as a geologist infers erosion from the absence of strata. What is required of the historian is not suspicion but a certain discipline of attention: the habit of asking, of every collection, who decided that it should exist and what was thought unworthy of the shelf.`,
`The grocery receipt, then, deserves its celebrity, though not for the reason usually supplied. It matters not because the unimportant sometimes proves useful, a notion that flatters our own luck, but because it reminds us that importance is a verdict rendered by the present, and that the present is a fallible judge.`],
q:[
['Purpose of a Detail',`The anecdote in paragraph 1 is introduced primarily in order to`,`offer a familiar story whose conventional moral the author will qualify`,[`prove that receipts are more valuable than treaties`,`criticise a particular archivist’s carelessness`,`explain how historical events are dated with precision`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 3, “facile” most nearly means`,`superficially simple`,[`carefully reasoned`,`emotionally charged`,`historically accurate`]],
['Inference',`The author implies in paragraph 2 that servants’ letters seldom survive because`,`earlier generations did not regard them as worth preserving`,[`servants rarely wrote letters`,`archivists preferred shorter documents`,`such letters were deliberately destroyed by their authors`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The comparison with a geologist in paragraph 3 suggests that historians can`,`draw conclusions from what is missing as well as from what remains`,[`date documents by examining their physical layers`,`avoid bias by studying rocks`,`safely ignore the gaps in the record`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`Which best describes the relationship of paragraph 3 to paragraph 2?`,`It resists a pessimistic inference that might be drawn from the preceding argument`,[`It supplies statistical evidence for the earlier claim`,`It introduces a topic unrelated to the earlier discussion`,`It restates the earlier claim in different terms`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The author most likely regards the notion that “the unimportant sometimes proves useful” as`,`a gratifying but inadequate lesson`,[`the central moral of the receipt story`,`an obvious falsehood`,`a rule that all historians ought to follow`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'terp',t:'The Interpreter’s Silence',lv:6,ps:[
`For eleven years I earned my living by speaking other people’s sentences in the first person. At conferences and tribunals, in hospital corridors and at border posts, I was the voice that said “I”, and the “I” was never mine. The profession demands a particular self-erasure: one must be audible without being present, accurate without being opinionated, like a pane of glass that takes pride in being overlooked.`,
`What they do not tell you in training is that glass, too, has a character. A phrase that is gentle in one language may arrive as a threat in another; a pause that signifies courtesy in one culture reads as evasion in the next. I learned, to my discomfort, that fidelity is not the same as literalness. To render a witness’s words with scrupulous exactness is sometimes to betray what she meant, and to betray what she meant is a lapse of faithfulness more grave than any error of vocabulary.`,
`I remember an elderly farmer, testifying about a dispute over a boundary wall, who described his neighbour as “a man who measures with a long tape”. Rendered word for word, it was nonsense; in his dialect it was a withering accusation of greed. I hesitated for perhaps a second, a second in which both parties stared at me, and then I chose to translate the meaning and not the metaphor. The magistrate nodded. The farmer, who understood some of the language, gave me a look I have never forgotten: not gratitude, exactly, but the wary respect of one craftsman for another.`,
`I no longer interpret professionally. But I find that I am more attentive to what people leave unsaid, and more suspicious of anyone, myself included, who claims to be merely a conduit.`],
q:[
["Writer's Technique",`The “pane of glass” simile in paragraph 1 is used to illustrate`,`the expectation that an interpreter be transparent and unobtrusive`,[`the fragility of professional reputations`,`the interpreter’s susceptibility to vanity`,`the physical barrier between the parties in a courtroom`]],
['Inference',`In paragraph 2, the author argues that “fidelity is not the same as literalness” because`,`a word-for-word rendering can distort the speaker’s intended meaning`,[`literal translation is technically impossible`,`faithful translators must avoid every metaphor`,`witnesses generally prefer simplified speech`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 3, “withering” most nearly means`,`devastatingly scornful`,[`gradually fading`,`cautiously polite`,`physically frail`]],
['Inference',`The farmer’s “look” at the end of paragraph 3 most plausibly conveys`,`tacit recognition that the interpreter had exercised skill and judgement`,[`resentment at having been misrepresented`,`embarrassment about his dialect`,`indifference to the proceedings`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The tone of the final paragraph is best described as`,`reflective and quietly self-critical`,[`triumphant and boastful`,`bitter and accusatory`,`detached and clinical`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`Which of the following best expresses the author’s revised view of the interpreter’s role?`,`Interpreters inevitably shape meaning and so bear a responsibility beyond mechanical accuracy`,[`Interpreters should refuse to translate idioms`,`The role is best performed by people without opinions`,`Interpreters are most useful when they interrupt`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'dist',t:'The Economics of Distraction',lv:7,ps:[
`It has become a commonplace that we live in an age of distraction, and a corresponding commonplace that the remedy is individual resolve: put the telephone in a drawer, cultivate habits of concentration, and the afflicted mind will mend itself. There is something to be said for such counsel, but it rests on a diagnosis that is at best partial, for it treats as a failing of character what is in large part a feature of design.`,
`Consider the economic structure of the attention marketplace. The platforms on which many of us spend our waking hours are, for the most part, not sold to their users; they are given away, and what is sold, to advertisers, is the users’ time. The commercial incentive is therefore not to satisfy but to retain, and retention is best secured by exploiting the very susceptibilities, the appetite for novelty, the dread of exclusion, that evolved in us for quite different purposes. To exhort an individual to resist a system engineered by thousands of specialists to defeat resistance is rather like advising a swimmer to defy a tide by effort of will.`,
`None of this absolves the individual entirely. Agency, however constrained, is not nothing, and there are circumstances in which a deliberate decision to disengage is both possible and wise. But an account of distraction that dwells exclusively on personal discipline resembles a theory of pollution that blames only the person who breathes. It flatters the powerful by relocating responsibility to the powerless, and it forecloses the more difficult question of whether the incentives themselves ought to be altered.`,
`The question, then, is not whether we should cultivate attention, which we plainly should, but whether we can afford to treat the cultivation as a private hobby when the conditions that undermine it are public and deliberate.`],
q:[
['Tone & Purpose',`The author’s primary purpose is to`,`challenge an explanation that attributes distraction chiefly to personal weakness`,[`advise readers on techniques for sustaining concentration`,`commend advertisers for supporting free services`,`predict the eventual decline of social platforms`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 2, “exhort” most nearly means`,`urge earnestly`,[`forbid sternly`,`reward generously`,`deceive subtly`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The image of the swimmer and the tide is used to suggest that`,`individual effort may prove inadequate against a powerful, deliberately engineered force`,[`swimming is a useful metaphor for sustained concentration`,`tides are too unpredictable to resist`,`resistance ought to be abandoned altogether`]],
['Inference',`Which of the following, if true, would most weaken the author’s argument in paragraph 2?`,`Users who disable a platform’s engagement features report no reduction in distraction`,[`Advertisers pay more for platforms that retain users for longer sessions`,`Platform designers frequently study human psychology`,`Some users already restrict the time they spend on screens`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The statement that the personal-discipline account “flatters the powerful by relocating responsibility to the powerless” implies that this account`,`diverts scrutiny from those who profit from distraction`,[`credits the powerful with exceptional self-discipline`,`holds that the powerless are blameless in every circumstance`,`denies that responsibility can ever be shared`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The author’s attitude towards individual agency is best described as`,`qualified: acknowledged as real yet judged insufficient`,[`dismissive: regarded as irrelevant to the problem`,`celebratory: regarded as the principal remedy`,`indifferent: regarded as a matter of private taste`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'marsh',t:'The Salt Marsh at Low Water',lv:5,ps:[
`At low water the marsh withdraws into an austere geometry of mud and channel, and the casual visitor, arriving with expectations of abundance, is apt to feel faintly cheated. The creeks, emptied of their tides, lie like dark veins in the grey flesh of the flats; the glasswort, that improbable succulent, flushes a rusty crimson at the margins; and over everything hangs a smell, sulphurous, rich, faintly rotten, that the unaccustomed nose takes for decay and the initiated for fertility.`,
`It is a smell, in fact, of enormous industry. The mud is a dense, oxygen-starved medium in which bacteria labour ceaselessly upon the debris carried in from land and sea, and the gas they release is the exhalation of a metabolism that underpins the whole community. Remove that labour and the marsh would drown in its own litter; permit it, and the litter is converted, with a thrift that no human economy has matched, into the nutrients on which crabs, worms and the grazing fish of the incoming tide depend.`,
`I have come here every spring for nearly thirty years, and what continues to astonish me is how little of this activity is visible. A pair of redshanks probes the shallows; a heron stands in the pose of a man waiting for a late train; nothing else moves. To the impatient eye the marsh is idle. It requires a different kind of looking, slow, unacquisitive, content to be teased by hints, before one begins to perceive that the stillness is merely the surface of an enormous patience.`],
q:[
['Vocabulary in Context',`In paragraph 1, “austere” most nearly conveys that the landscape at low water appears`,`stark and unadorned`,[`luxuriant and ornate`,`hostile and dangerous`,`neglected and abandoned`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The “unaccustomed nose” and “the initiated” interpret the smell differently in order to illustrate that`,`perception depends upon knowledge and experience`,[`smells are objectively unpleasant`,`experts cannot detect decay`,`the marsh has been polluted`]],
['Literal Comprehension',`According to paragraph 2, the bacteria in the mud are important because they`,`convert accumulated debris into nutrients that sustain other organisms`,[`strip all oxygen from the incoming tide`,`prevent crabs from entering the marsh`,`produce the marsh’s characteristic colours`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The comparison of the heron to “a man waiting for a late train” (paragraph 3) primarily serves to`,`lend the bird a wry, humanising patience`,[`suggest that the heron has lost its way`,`imply that the marsh lies near a railway`,`criticise the impatience of visitors`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`The word “unacquisitive” in paragraph 3 describes a manner of looking that is`,`free of any wish to possess or extract something`,[`wholly indifferent to detail`,`rapid and superficial`,`technically precise`]],
['Inference',`The final sentence suggests that the author has come to regard the marsh’s stillness as`,`a deceptive surface over sustained, quiet activity`,[`evidence that nothing of interest occurs there`,`a symptom of ecological decline`,`proof of the visitor’s impatience`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'lib',t:'A Quiet Revolution in Brick',lv:7,ps:[
`The library that opened this spring on the old tannery site is, by the standards of contemporary civic architecture, almost aggressively modest. It declines the flourishes with which cities customarily advertise their ambitions: there is no atrium, no cantilevered auditorium, no façade of shimmering metal. What there is instead is brick, thousands upon thousands of hand-laid bricks in a dull and varying red, assembled into a long low building that appears, from the street, scarcely to be a building at all.`,
`Such restraint is not, one soon perceives, timidity. The architects have bent their ingenuity towards the experience of the reader and not the admiration of the passer-by. Reading rooms are lit by narrow clerestory windows that cast an even, shadowless light across the tables; the ceilings are low enough to induce a sense of shelter; and the brickwork absorbs sound with such thoroughness that the place hums with a silence that feels earned rather than enforced. One is reminded that the finest buildings are often those one notices least.`,
`If the design has a defect, it is that its virtues are largely invisible to anyone who does not enter. The exterior, scrupulously self-effacing, gives no hint of the interior’s quiet drama, and one suspects that a library so reticent may struggle to persuade the indifferent to cross its threshold. It is a paradox that the architects may not have wholly resolved: a building that exists to invite, yet declines to beckon.`,
`Still, it would be churlish to quarrel overmuch. In an era when public buildings so often compete to be photographed, here is one content merely to be used, and the difference, I think, will be felt for decades after the novelty of its neighbours has faded.`],
q:[
["Writer's Technique",`The phrase “almost aggressively modest” (paragraph 1) is notable chiefly because it`,`pairs two seemingly conflicting terms to stress the building’s determined restraint`,[`suggests that the reviewer is hostile to modesty`,`implies that the architects were careless`,`indicates that the building is unattractive`]],
['Literal Comprehension',`According to paragraph 2, the architects’ priority was`,`the comfort and concentration of the library’s readers`,[`the building’s visibility from the street`,`the economy of the materials used`,`the preservation of the tannery’s history`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 3, “reticent” most nearly means`,`disinclined to reveal itself`,[`ostentatious`,`recently completed`,`excessively spacious`]],
['Inference',`The “paradox” identified in paragraph 3 is that the building`,`exists to attract visitors yet is designed in a way that does little to draw them in`,[`is both old and new in appearance`,`is quiet but also very large`,`uses costly materials in an inexpensive style`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The reviewer’s overall assessment is best described as`,`admiring, with a reservation`,[`enthusiastic without qualification`,`grudging and critical`,`indifferent`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`The word “churlish” (paragraph 4) suggests that quarrelling over the design would be`,`ungenerous and ill-mannered`,[`scholarly and rigorous`,`unavoidable and just`,`humorous and light-hearted`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'gift',t:'The Arithmetic of Gifts',lv:7,ps:[
`A gift, we are taught, is the antithesis of a transaction. The one is freely given, the other exacted; the one expresses affection, the other merely settles an account. This neat opposition has the authority of common sense, and anthropologists have spent the better part of a century quietly demonstrating that it is wrong.`,
`In many societies studied by ethnographers, the exchange of gifts is not a sentimental supplement to economic life but a principal engine of it. To receive a gift is to incur an obligation, and the obligation is rarely discharged immediately: the recipient must wait, lest a prompt return suggest that the gift was unwelcome as a burden, and must then reciprocate with something a little more generous, so that the debt, like a ball in play, passes back to the original giver. The interval between gift and counter-gift is not an inefficiency; it is the very mechanism by which relationships are sustained.`,
`The implications are uncomfortable for those who prefer their generosity pure. If gifts bind, then to give is to exert a species of power, and to refuse a gift is to refuse the bond it proposes. Hence the ritual protest, familiar in our own manners, with which a recipient demurs (“You shouldn’t have!”) before accepting with evident pleasure; the protest acknowledges, however playfully, that something more than an object is changing hands.`,
`None of which proves that generosity is a fraud. It suggests rather that the sincere and the strategic are not the opposites we imagine, and that a motive need not be pure to be genuine. The grandmother who knits a scarf is neither cynic nor saint; she is a participant, wholly and willingly, in the oldest system of credit that humanity has devised.`],
q:[
['Inference',`The “neat opposition” referred to in paragraph 1 is the belief that`,`gifts and transactions are fundamentally opposed`,[`affection can be measured in economic terms`,`anthropologists distrust common sense`,`gifts are always reciprocated at once`]],
['Literal Comprehension',`According to paragraph 2, the interval before a return gift is significant because it`,`helps to sustain the relationship between the parties`,[`ensures that the return gift costs less`,`shows that the original gift was unwelcome`,`prevents the debt from ever being repaid`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 3, “demurs” most nearly means`,`raises a mild objection`,[`accepts without hesitation`,`feigns ignorance`,`responds with anger`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The “ritual protest” is cited as evidence that`,`even everyday manners acknowledge the obligations that gifts create`,[`people dislike receiving presents`,`most gifts are insincere`,`manners vary little between cultures`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The simile of “a ball in play” in paragraph 2 conveys that the obligation`,`keeps returning to the giver rather than ending with the recipient`,[`is a trivial game`,`should be discarded as quickly as possible`,`cannot be understood by outsiders`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The author’s concluding position is that`,`a gift may be both sincere and strategic without contradiction`,[`generosity is fundamentally fraudulent`,`gifts ought to be avoided`,`only sentimental gifts are genuine`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'ant',t:'The Patient Observer',lv:5,ps:[
`Hedda Varnholt never intended to become a scientist. A schoolmistress in a remote mountain parish, she took up the study of ants in the winter of her thirty-fourth year for the unglamorous reason that the children in her charge had exhausted her patience and the garden was the only quiet place she could find. Her equipment was a magnifying glass, a notebook and a stool; her sole advantage, as she later conceded, was a capacity to sit still for a very long time without feeling that she was wasting it.`,
`From those hours of immobility came a discovery that the learned societies of her day had considered improbable. The ants she watched did not, as received opinion held, follow a leader; they followed one another’s traces, each insect reinforcing or abandoning a path according to the evidence of its own antennae. Order, it appeared, required no commander. It emerged, unbidden, from the accumulated choices of thousands of creatures, none of whom grasped the design to which they contributed.`,
`The reception of her findings was predictable. Professors who had never knelt in a garden pronounced her observations anecdotal; a distinguished entomologist, in a letter that she kept for the remainder of her life, advised her to leave speculation to those equipped for it. She did not reply. She went on watching, and she went on writing, and it was left to a later generation, armed with instruments she could not have imagined, to confirm that her stool in the garden had been a better observatory than their laboratories.`],
q:[
['Inference',`The reason Varnholt began studying ants, as described in paragraph 1, was`,`largely circumstantial rather than the product of scientific ambition`,[`a commission from a learned society`,`a long-standing passion for entomology`,`a wish to supplement her teaching salary`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 1, “conceded” most nearly means`,`admitted reluctantly`,[`boasted`,`denied`,`forgot`]],
['Literal Comprehension',`Which statement best summarises Varnholt’s discovery in paragraph 2?`,`Collective order can arise without any directing leader`,[`Ants obey a single dominant insect`,`Antennae are used only for feeding`,`Paths are determined by the weather`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The word “unbidden” in paragraph 2 emphasises that the order`,`arose spontaneously, without having been commanded`,[`was imposed from above`,`was unwelcome to the ants`,`was invisible to the observer`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The author’s attitude towards the professors in paragraph 3 is best described as`,`mildly ironic, hinting that their scorn was unearned`,[`respectful of their caution`,`indifferent to their opinion`,`envious of their equipment`]],
['Inference',`The final sentence implies that`,`careful, prolonged observation can outperform sophisticated facilities`,[`laboratories are unnecessary for any science`,`later researchers disproved her findings`,`garden stools are better than microscopes`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'port',t:'Arrival',lv:6,ps:[
`Airports are the cathedrals of a faithless age, and like cathedrals they are built to induce a particular mood: not reverence, exactly, but a suspended, receptive vagueness in which the traveller consents to be directed. The corridors curve to discourage the idea of destination; the lighting, which belongs to no hour, erases the time of day; and the announcements, delivered in a voice of studied serenity, reach us as the liturgy of an order we do not quite believe in but dare not defy.`,
`It is tempting to deplore all this as a kind of sedation, and many do. Yet I have come to suspect that the airport’s blandness is not a failure of imagination but its triumph. A place that must serve, in a single day, travellers from every culture and temper cannot afford idiosyncrasy; character is a luxury of those who stay. What looks like emptiness is in truth a hospitality of a peculiar sort, one that asks nothing of us, expects no knowledge of local custom, and permits each person to remain, for a few hours, almost wholly anonymous.`,
`There is, however, a price. The sensation of being everywhere is difficult to distinguish from the sensation of being nowhere, and I have noticed, on long layovers, how readily the mind loosens its moorings. I find myself composing letters I shall never send, rehearsing conversations with people I have not seen in years, as though the suspension of place had licensed a suspension of the self’s customary reticences. It is only when the departure board flickers and my gate is announced that I am returned, with something like relief, to the burdens of being somebody in particular.`],
q:[
["Writer's Technique",`The comparison of airports to cathedrals in paragraph 1 primarily suggests that airports`,`are designed to cultivate a deliberately compliant frame of mind in visitors`,[`are built to honour religious traditions`,`rival cathedrals in architectural splendour`,`attract travellers who are deeply devout`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 2, “idiosyncrasy” most nearly means`,`distinctive individual quirkiness`,[`systematic efficiency`,`financial extravagance`,`cultural tolerance`]],
['Inference',`The author’s argument in paragraph 2 is that airport blandness`,`reflects a practical necessity and a kind of unobtrusive hospitality`,[`demonstrates the architects’ lack of talent`,`discourages travellers from returning`,`exists to sedate customers deliberately`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The “price” referred to in paragraph 3 is`,`a loosening of one’s sense of place and identity`,[`the cost of airport meals`,`the missed connections of long layovers`,`the loss of correspondence in transit`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`In paragraph 3, “moorings” is used figuratively to mean`,`the anchors that keep one’s thoughts steady`,[`the docks at which ships lie`,`the rules of air travel`,`the places where letters are posted`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`Overall, the author’s attitude towards airports is best described as`,`thoughtfully ambivalent`,[`scornfully dismissive`,`uncritically admiring`,`bored and detached`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'orch',t:'The Orchard in Winter',lv:6,ps:[
`Nothing here is promised. The bare boughs hold<br>their arms against the sky like prisoners<br>who have forgotten what the sentence was,<br>and the frost has taken even the last fruit.`,
`Yet look: along each twig, a tight, grey bud,<br>small as a clenched fist, and not yet sure<br>whether to open. Who told them to prepare?<br>No voice has called. They simply keep the faith.`,
`I walk between the rows with cold, bare hands<br>and think of all the sums I could not do,<br>the debts postponed, the words left unsaid, and wonder<br>how patience learns its own geography.`],
q:[
["Writer's Technique",`In stanza 1, the bare boughs are compared to prisoners to suggest that the trees appear`,`confined and resigned, uncertain of their release`,[`violent and rebellious`,`joyful and celebratory`,`strong and menacing`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`The phrase “keep the faith” in stanza 2 suggests that the buds`,`persist in trusting a future they cannot see`,[`obey a religious ritual`,`mistake winter for spring`,`regret the loss of their fruit`]],
['Inference',`The question “Who told them to prepare?” implies that the buds’ readiness is`,`instinctive, arising without any outside instruction`,[`the result of careful gardening`,`a response to human voices`,`proof of a deliberate design`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The shift from the orchard to “all the sums I could not do” in stanza 3 serves to`,`link the trees’ patience to the speaker’s own unresolved concerns`,[`introduce an unrelated memory`,`criticise the speaker’s education`,`suggest that winter is fatal to orchards`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The mood of the poem as a whole is best described as`,`quietly hopeful beneath its bleakness`,[`frenzied and anxious`,`triumphant and celebratory`,`coldly detached`]],
["Writer's Technique",`The figurative use of “geography” in the final line suggests that patience`,`has a landscape of its own that must be learned and traversed`,[`is a subject taught in schools`,`depends upon accurate maps`,`varies from one country to another`]]]});
PSG.push({id:'reply',t:'On the Tyranny of Reply-All',lv:6,ps:[
`There is, in every modern office, an instrument of torture so refined that its victims administer it to one another daily and call the practice courtesy. I refer, of course, to the reply-all button. Its premise is admirable: that no colleague should be denied the benefit of one’s thoughts. Its consequence is that a message announcing the retirement of a beloved accountant arrives, within the hour, in the inboxes of four hundred people, each of whom has been furnished with another person’s gratitude and no means of escape.`,
`The mechanism is simple and, once understood, inexorable. The first replier, moved by genuine warmth, writes “Congratulations!” Having done so, the second feels it would be churlish to remain silent; the third, observing that a dozen have already spoken, concludes that silence has become conspicuous; and by mid-afternoon the entire company is engaged in an elaborate ritual of mutual reassurance, in which the one thing nobody can afford is to be seen not to have said anything.`,
`Some have proposed technical remedies, among them warning boxes, delay timers and the banning of the offending button. I confess to being unmoved. The difficulty is not the button but the person, who has never been able to resist the pleasure of being included in a crowd, and who will, if denied one avenue, discover another. The remedy, if there is one, lies in the humbler virtue of leaving unsaid what has already been said better by someone else.`],
q:[
["Writer's Technique",`The description of the reply-all button as “an instrument of torture” is an example of`,`humorous hyperbole used to ridicule a minor annoyance`,[`a literal description of a workplace hazard`,`understated criticism of an employer`,`a technical explanation of software design`]],
['Vocabulary in Context',`As used in paragraph 2, “inexorable” most nearly means`,`impossible to stop or resist`,[`pleasantly surprising`,`easily reversed`,`needlessly complicated`]],
['Inference',`According to paragraph 2, the second person replies mainly because`,`silence would seem ungracious after the first reply`,[`they have been instructed to respond`,`they wish to correct an error`,`they dislike the retiring accountant`]],
['Purpose of a Detail',`The author mentions “warning boxes, delay timers and the banning of the offending button” in order to`,`list proposed solutions that the author considers beside the point`,[`recommend the solutions to readers`,`explain how the software works`,`show that managers are incompetent`]],
['Tone & Purpose',`The tone of the passage is best described as`,`wry and gently mocking`,[`earnest and technical`,`furious and accusatory`,`solemn and nostalgic`]],
['Inference',`The author’s concluding suggestion is that the problem would be best solved by`,`individual restraint in refraining from redundant replies`,[`stronger software controls`,`a company-wide ban on email`,`more frequent congratulations`]]]});
